## Quick Reference Card

**One-time clone (if needed):**
```powershell
Set-Location -LiteralPath "PARENT_FOLDER_PATH"
git clone https://github.com/USERNAME/REPO_NAME.git
```

**Pull updates from one folder (use this every time):**
```powershell
Set-Location -LiteralPath "PATH_TO_YOUR_REPO"
git fetch origin
git checkout origin/main -- FOLDER_NAME/
```

**Check where you are:**
```powershell
pwd
```

**Check if you're in a Git repo:**
```powershell
git status
```

**See your remotes:**
```powershell
git remote -v
```

**See remote branches:**
```powershell
git branch -r
```

---

## What NOT to Do

**DO NOT use `git sparse-checkout set FOLDER_NAME`** if you want to keep your other local files. This command DELETES everything except the folder you specify. It's destructive.

**DO NOT use `git pull`** if you only want to update one folder. This pulls everything.

---

## Summary

To safely update one folder from a repo without touching anything else:

```powershell
Set-Location -LiteralPath "PATH_TO_YOUR_REPO"
git fetch origin
git checkout origin/main -- FOLDER_NAME/
```

That's it. Three commands. Safe and targeted.
